ccess tokens.An _MCP client_ acts as an OAuth 2.1 client, making protected resource requests on behalf of a resource owner.The _authorization s"}, {"cite_id": "W4", "url": "https://authzed.com/learn/mcp-oauth-2-1-authentication", "title": "MCP and OAuth 2.1: How authentication works for MCP servers", "domain": "authzed.com", "score": 0.8396, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-27T16:58:16.923Z", "snippet": "Learn what the MCP OAuth 2.1 specification requires, why proxy workarounds cause account takeovers, and where authentication stops and authorization must begin.\n\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) specification standardizes remote agent access on OAuth 2.1, requiring PKCE, dynamic "}]}, "client_ms": 8289}
